Each recipe names where its code comes from: the [example site](https://github.com/micacms/mica/tree/main/examples/company-site), which Mica's CI builds, a test that CI runs, the [starter](https://github.com/micacms/mica/tree/main/examples/starter), or an Astro option.

## Show an image with its alt text

Give the field `alt`. Astro then gets `{ src, alt }`, and Astro's `<Image>` takes both.

```ts
heroImage: media({ label: "Hero image", accept: "image", alt: "required" }),
```

```astro
---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
const { heroImage } = page.data;
---
{heroImage && <Image {...heroImage} widths={[800, 1200, 1800]} sizes="92vw" />}
```

From `src/mica.ts` and `src/pages/[...slug].astro` of the example. With `alt: "required"`, an editor cannot publish an image without alt text. Use `"optional"` for an image that is only decoration: an empty alt text is `""`.

## Show related entries

A relation stores the slug of the other entry. `@micacms/astro/relations` gets the entries.

```astro
---
import { related, relatedAll } from "@micacms/astro/relations";
const writer = await related("team", article.data.author);
const members = await relatedAll("team", project.data.team);
---
{writer && <a href={`/team/${writer.id}/`}>{writer.data.name}</a>}
```

From `src/pages/news/[slug].astro` and `src/pages/projects/[slug].astro` of the example. To show a relation from the other side, for example the projects of one person, filter the other collection:

```ts
const projects = (await getCollection("projects")).filter((project) => project.data.team.includes(member.id));
```

For a relation to data that Mica does not edit, see [Reference data](/docs/reference-data/).

## Show images and tables in rich text

With `toolbar: "full"`, or `"table"` and `"image"` in a list, editors add tables and images to the text. A table needs no code. An image needs the data of the entry, so that it gets its file and its address on the website. Give `renderRichText` the entry's data as the second argument. It is async:

```astro
<RichText text={body} from={page.data} />
```

```astro
---
import { renderRichText, type RichTextSource } from "@micacms/astro";
interface Props { text: string | null; from?: RichTextSource | null }
const { text, from } = Astro.props;
const html = await renderRichText(text, from);
---
<div set:html={html} />
```

Shortened from `src/pages/[...slug].astro` and `src/components/RichText.astro` of the example. Astro's image service makes each image smaller: a `srcset` in 480, 800, 1200 and 1600 px as WebP, up to the width of the original. The site needs `sharp`, as for `<Image>`. `{ optimize: false }` as the third argument writes the original. Style `table` and `img` in your rich-text styles.

## Use the order of the editor

When a collection is `sortable`, editors put its entries in an order. `defaultSort` sets the order too. Astro does not sort by it, so sort on your page with `inEditorOrder`:

```ts
import { inEditorOrder } from "@micacms/astro";

export async function getTeam() {
  const items = await getCollection("team");
  return [...items].sort(inEditorOrder);
}
```

From `src/lib/content.ts` of the example.

## Read the settings

A singleton is an Astro collection with one entry. `getSingleton` reads it with the type of the model:

```ts
import { getSingleton } from "@micacms/astro";
import { site } from "./mica";

const settings = await getSingleton(site, "site");
```

It stops the build with a clear message when the settings are not saved yet. The example reads each singleton in `src/lib/content.ts`.

## Show a place on a map

A `coordinates({ map: "liberty" })` field gives editors a map to click on. On the website, `LocationMap` draws the same place. It has no styles of its own: give it a size.

```astro
---
import { LocationMap } from "@micacms/astro/components";
---
<LocationMap
  place={venue.place}
  map="positron"
  label={`Map: ${venue.name}`}
  class="block h-56 w-full overflow-hidden"
/>
```

From `src/pages/events/[slug].astro` of the example. Add `maplibre-gl` to the dependencies of the website. The map loads when it comes near the screen. Until then, and without JavaScript, the element holds a link to the place on OpenStreetMap. The zoom is the one that the editor saved. `mapView()` from `@micacms/astro` gives the same view for a map that you draw yourself.

## Read a file at build time

An image reaches Astro as an image. Another file, such as a GPX track, reaches Astro as a path from the content root. `mediaFile` gives the file on disk:

```ts
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { mediaFile } from "@micacms/astro";
import { site } from "./mica";

const track = await readFile(mediaFile(site, entry.data.track), "utf8");
```

The example copies the downloads of its projects into the built site in `src/pages/files/[...path].ts`.

## Add your own rich-text tag

Declare the tag in a Markdoc configuration, and give it to `renderRichText`. A tag that the configuration does not declare gives no output.

```ts
import Markdoc from "@markdoc/markdoc";
import { renderRichText } from "@micacms/astro";

const config = {
  tags: {
    callout: {
      render: "aside",
      attributes: { type: { type: String, default: "note", matches: ["note", "warning"] } },
      transform(node: Markdoc.Node, config: Markdoc.Config) {
        const attributes = node.transformAttributes(config);
        return new Markdoc.Tag("aside", { class: `callout callout-${attributes.type}` }, node.transformChildren(config));
      },
    },
  },
};

const html = await renderRichText(entry.data.body, entry.data, { config });
```

```markdoc {% process=false %}
{% callout type="warning" %}
Book a time **before** you visit.
{% /callout %}
```

The test `packages/astro/test/richtext.test.ts` renders this configuration. The editor cannot show a tag that it does not know, so it shows a rich-text field with such a tag read-only. Add the tag in the `.mdoc` file, and use it for content that changes seldom.

## Check the content in CI

```yaml
- run: npm ci
# Every entry as Mica and the website read it: fields, media, links and layout.
- run: npx --no mica check
# In CI, the build fails when content/.mica/schema.json is not the model in the code.
- run: npm run build
```

From `.github/workflows/check.yml` of the starter, which runs in each new site. `mica check` stops the job at an error. The build stops at a model change without its `schema.json`: the editor would show the old model. The build does the same for the index of a [reference set](/docs/reference-data/). A step with `git diff --exit-code content/.mica/schema.json` after the build is not needed any more.

## Change the URL of an entry

The slug is the URL. When an editor changes it, Mica offers to update the links in other entries (on by default), and it tells the editor that old links from outside need a redirect. Add the redirect in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  redirects: {
    "/news/old-name/": "/news/new-name/",
  },
});
```

`redirects` is an [Astro option](https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/#redirects). On a static host, Astro writes a small page that sends the visitor on. Some hosts can also use a redirect file.

## Questions

**Can two editors work at the same time?** Yes. All editors of a site see the saved changes. When two editors change the same entry, the second save shows both versions, and the editor chooses.

**Where are the changes before Publish?** They are files in the working tree that are not committed: in your checkout for the local editor, or in the clone on the data volume for a self-hosted Mica.

**Does Mica need a database?** No. Git holds the content and its history.

**Can I use Mica without Astro?** The content is JSON, Markdoc and media files, so other tools can read it. Mica gives a loader for Astro only.

**How do I undo a publish?** Revert the commit with Git. See [Scope](/docs/scope/).

**Why can I not rename a media file?** An entry finds a file by its path, so a rename changes each entry that uses it. Upload the file again and change the entries. You can delete a file when no entry uses it.

**Can I see a page before I publish it?** With the local editor, yes: run `astro dev` beside it. A self-hosted Mica has no preview. See [Preview changes](/docs/previews/).

**Can the site hold thousands of photos?** Yes. Keep them in Git LFS. See [Large files with Git LFS](/docs/git-lfs/).

**Is there more than one language?** See [Several languages](/docs/multilingual/).
